Recognized as a landmark figure with global significance (referenced in 0 Wikipedia editions). Relevant to political systems and geopolitics. Its legacy: Shaped political institutions and governance practices.", "newValue": "Miles de Noyers (died 1350), a preeminent French diplomat and Grand Panetier of France, wielded considerable influence during the turbulent reign of King Philip VI of Valois. His career, spanning the early decades of the Hundred Years' War, positioned him at the nexus of Anglo-French geopolitical tensions and the evolving administrative structures of the French monarchy. De Noyers's strategic acumen and diplomatic missions were instrumental in navigating the complex alliances and conflicts that defined 14th-century European politics, particularly concerning the nascent Valois claim to the French throne and the ongoing disputes with England.

As Grand Panetier, a high office of the French Crown, Miles de Noyers was responsible for royal provisions but more significantly served as a key advisor and negotiator for Philip VI. His diplomatic efforts included critical embassies to the Papal Court in Avignon and negotiations with various feudal lords, aiming to consolidate royal authority and secure support against Edward III of England. He played a direct role in the political maneuvering surrounding the outbreak of the Hundred Years' War, notably advocating for French interests in Flanders and Aquitaine. His influence extended to military administration, where he contributed to the logistical organization of French forces in early campaigns.

